    "turn_idx": 167,
    "turn_text": "stage. But I had danced most of my life. I was a gymnast. I was a state champion gymnast, national finalist and vault. And so that was what I thought I was going to do. And then, you know, injuries happened. So you had to find something else. And I was really interested in public health. Ended up getting a public health degree and a law degree. So I could work on particularly reproductive health issues and policy settings. And then. When my dad was in the state legislature and was turned out, I thought, OK, that could be interesting. He was encouraging me to run. I didn't think it was for me. And when I saw who was ultimately saying that they wanted to run, I felt like they would be fine. But I felt like they wanted to have the title. They didn't want to serve the community. And so I ran. I felt very responsible to my community. They raised me. They raised my sister and I. And they needed someone who was going to actually take care of them and care about them. So I ran. I was in the State House for eight years, the Minority Leader for three, and then decided to run for Congress. And the decision to run for Congress was sort of from my time as Minority Leader sitting on people's couches, begging them to run in districts that we knew were going to be very difficult to run and win in because they were gerrymandering, but giving them the a whole, you know, we need you for democracy. Democracy only works if there's two parties who are engaged in exchange of ideas. And if you're not on the ballot and they have no one else, they just think that there's just one way we need you. And then people came to me and said, you want to run for Congress? And I was a little hesitant. And then they gave me that same speech I was given. I was like, oh, man, OK, well, Yeah, I should probably be practicing what I preach. And so that's how I ended up running for Congress and also just really feeling committed and connected to the community and responsible for making sure that we had good government and someone who cared.",
