  {
    "episode_id": 74691,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74691.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 179,
    "turn_text": "Yes, the Black Maternal Health Caucus put forth the Mom and Abyss bill very early in the Congress, which deals with transportation issues, housing issues. And then the first version of the Mom and Abyss was a little bit more clinical in getting maternal review boards and some funding and encouraging. We saw this from the vice president with extending Medicaid care beyond just two months, I believe it was into the full year after pregnancy. So we've done some of these things, but we're now starting to talk a little bit more about reproduction and reproductive health care. And the Dobbs decision has given us an opportunity to do so. And it has also shown us some failures of what happens when we are not talking about abortion care. The thing that my colleagues didn't want to talk about before, because it was too controversial, is now creating situations where we see women dying. women in Georgia, we just found out dying because they show up at the emergency department, miscarrying, and the doctors don't know what to do. They're afraid to get prosecuted. So they just say, well, come back when you're septic. I mean, what kind of thing is that to say? And they come back and it's too late and now they're dying. You have women like Brittany Watson, Ohio, who she went to the emergency room three times. They sent her home, but they didn't refer her to an OBGYN. They referred her for prosecution. You have a 10-year-old rape victim in Ohio because of abortion ban. Couldn't get an abortion in the state of Ohio. 10-year-old had to flee the state to get the care that they needed. And so now we are trying to reinstate Roe. Uh, we have an entala, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act resolution that says when somebody shows up in an emergency or an active labor, you have to treat them. You can't just let them die and suffer. Uh, we have the, uh, contraception for all act, which is again, a part of the contraceptive and the maternal health process to decide when you want to have a pregnancy. So we have several pieces of legislation. We just don't have 218 people willing to vote in favor of it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "In Georgia, women presenting with miscarriages are being denied treatment due to fear of legal prosecution.",
        "confidence": 0.98
